-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Numerical Stability**: The product term in transmittance $T_k$ can underflow if many Gaussians have high opacity. Standard epsilon guards should be applied [G].
-- **Sorting**: Correct front-to-back sorting is critical for the volume rendering equation to hold. Incorrect sorting leads to incorrect color and depth [S1].
-- **Surface Extraction**: GOF derives a level-set surface from accumulated opacity. This requires careful thresholding of the opacity field to extract a clean mesh, as vanilla 3DGS geometry is an emergent property of floating ellipsoids [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Degenerate Gaussians:** The parent article notes the challenge of handling degenerate cases where the determinant of the covariance matrix approaches zero [13401-ray-splat-intersection-algorithms.md]. GOF relies on the ray-tracing intersection to handle this, but specific numerical guards are not detailed in the sources [S1].
+*   **Sorting:** Gaussians must be sorted by intersection depth $t$ for correct front-to-back compositing [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Accuracy**: Provides more accurate depth and surface normals compared to standard 3DGS due to ray-tracing-style intersection [S2, unofficial].
-- **Complexity**: Analytically evaluating Gaussian contributions along rays is computationally more expensive than 2D projection rasterization used in 3DGS [S1].
-- **Use Case**: Preferred when high-quality geometric reconstruction, mesh extraction, or accurate depth is required, such as for physics simulation or DCC pipelines [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Not given in the sources.
+*   **Accuracy:** Provides accurate depth estimation compared to 3DGS projection [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Surface Extraction:** Enables the derivation of level-set surfaces from accumulated opacity [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The source does not provide specific timing or memory benchmarks. It is noted as being slower than standard 3DGS rasterization due to the ray-tracing-style intersection, but faster than per-pixel sort methods like PSGS [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Use Cases:** Suitable when accurate geometry and mesh extraction are required, as vanilla 3DGS geometry is an emergent property and produces floating ellipsoids [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) was introduced by Yang et al. in 2024 [S1]. It builds upon the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [KKLD23] to address the lack of explicit geometric constraints in vanilla 3DGS, which produces floating ellipsoids rather than surfaces [S2, unofficial]. GOF enables the derivation of level-set surfaces from accumulated opacity, facilitating better mesh extraction and geometric regularization [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) was introduced by Yang et al. [YSG24] [S1].
+*   **Development:** It builds upon the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [KKLD23] to address the lack of explicit geometric constraints in vanilla 3DGS [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in multiview geometric regularization strategies to improve radiance field accuracy [S1]. It is often compared with other variants like Mini-Splatting, StopThePop, and 2DGS [S2, unofficial].
 
